'' Array indexing, measured against fbc -- see docs/internals/phase-6-arrays.md.
''
''     address = base + SUM( index_k * stride_k ) - SUM( lbound_k * stride_k )
''
'' row-major, so the LAST dimension has stride elemSize and each earlier one is scaled by
'' the element counts of every dimension after it. fbc lowers b(i,i) for
'' b(1 to 3, 1 to 4) to base + 20i - 20.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/array.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' Storage, not one element. This was 'local i32 a' -- four bytes for forty -- and went
'' unnoticed because indexing was not lowered to write through it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: local agg a size 40
'' EXPECT-OUT: local agg b size 48

'' The assertions name VALUES, not temp numbers: the golden already pins the exact text,
'' and a %N here breaks whenever an unrelated instruction is added ahead of it.
''
'' One dimension, lbound 0: stride 4, and no bias to subtract.
'' EXPECT-OUT: ptr.add @a,

'' Two dimensions, lbound 1 on both: strides 16 and 4, and a bias of 1*16 + 1*4 = 20
'' subtracted ONCE for the whole subscript rather than per dimension.
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 16
'' EXPECT-OUT: const i64 20
'' EXPECT-OUT: ptr.add @b,

'' ptr.add is its OWN opcode rather than add, so nothing downstream can read the offset
'' as an integer to be scaled again by an element size.
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/array.out
